---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { DIRT_MATERIALS } from '../calculators/dirt';
import { fillDirtResult } from '../lib/sitework';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const ex = fillDirtResult(20 * 10 * 1, 20, 1.2, 10);
const holes = [
  { name: 'Low spot, 10 × 10 ft, 6 in deep', ft3: 10 * 10 * 0.5 },
  { name: 'Yard area, 20 × 30 ft, 6 in deep', ft3: 20 * 30 * 0.5 },
  { name: 'Pool or pond fill, 15 × 30 ft, 4 ft deep', ft3: 15 * 30 * 4 },
  { name: 'Raise a lot, 50 × 100 ft, 1 ft', ft3: 50 * 100 },
].map((h) => ({ ...h, r: fillDirtResult(h.ft3, 20, 1.2, 10) }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="dirt-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Dirt Calculator: Fill Dirt in Cubic Yards, Tons & Truckloads"
  description="Free dirt calculator. Enter the area and depth to fill and get the cubic yards of fill dirt to order, with extra for compaction, plus tons and dump truck loads."
  h1="Dirt Calculator"
  updated="2026-10-24"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find out how much fill dirt to order to fill a hole, level a low spot, or raise a yard. The calculator adds the extra
    you need for compaction and turns it into tons and truckloads.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate fill dirt</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure the area in feet. For a round hole, choose Circle; for an odd yard, add several shapes to a project.</li>
    <li>Measure the depth to fill, from the bottom of the hole to the finished grade, and convert to feet.</li>
    <li>Multiply length × width × depth for cubic feet, then divide by 27 for cubic yards.</li>
    <li>Add 10–25% for compaction: loose dirt shrinks when it is tamped or rolled.</li>
    <li>Multiply cubic yards by the weight of the dirt, about 1.2 tons per yard dry, for tons.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: raising a 20 × 10 ft area by 12 inches</h2>
  <p>
    20 × 10 × 1 = 200 cubic feet, or {fmt(ex.compactedYd3)} cubic yards once compacted. With 20% extra for a plate
    compactor, order <strong>{fmt(ex.cubicYards)} cubic yards</strong> of loose fill, which weighs about {fmt(ex.tons)} tons.
    That fits in one tandem dump truck.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compaction">How much extra for compaction?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>How the fill is placed</th><th class="num">Extra to order</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Dumped and raked, left to settle</td><td class="num">0–10%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tamped by hand in layers</td><td class="num">10–15%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plate compactor in 4–6 in lifts</td><td class="num">15–20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Clay soil or heavy roller compaction</td><td class="num">20–25%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The exact shrink depends on the soil and its moisture. Under a slab, shed, or patio, compact in thin lifts and use the
    higher number; under a lawn, light tamping is enough.
  </p>

  <h2 id="weight">How much does a yard of dirt weigh?</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th class="num">Tons per cubic yard</th><th class="num">Pounds per cubic yard</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{DIRT_MATERIALS.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.label}</td><td class="num">{m.tonsPerYd3}</td><td class="num">{(m.tonsPerYd3 * 2000).toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Wet dirt can weigh far more than dry. If your supplier sells by the ton, ask for their weight per yard and use Custom weight.</p>

  <h2 id="table">Fill dirt for common jobs</h2>
  <p>20% extra for compaction, dry fill at 1.2 tons per yard, tandem trucks of about 10 yards.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th class="num">Cubic yards</th><th class="num">Tons</th><th class="num">Truckloads</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{holes.map((h) => <tr><td>{h.name}</td><td class="num">{fmt(h.r.cubicYards, 1)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(h.r.tons, 1)}</td><td class="num">{h.r.truckloads}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="fill-vs-topsoil">Fill dirt or topsoil?</h2>
  <p>
    Fill dirt is subsoil with little organic matter. It compacts well and does not rot, so it is the right choice for
    filling holes, grading, and building up a base. It is poor for growing. Fill to within 4–6 inches of the final grade,
    then finish with topsoil if grass or plants will go on top: the <a href="/topsoil-calculator/">topsoil calculator</a>
    handles that layer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many cubic yards are in a dump truck?</h3>
  <p>
    A single-axle dump truck carries about 5 cubic yards and a tandem about 10, sometimes up to 14 for light material. Wet
    dirt can hit the weight limit before the bed is full, so ask the hauler.
  </p>
  <h3>How much fill dirt do I need to fill a hole?</h3>
  <p>
    Measure the hole's width, length, and depth, multiply them, and divide by 27 for cubic yards. For a round hole use π ×
    radius² × depth. Then add 10–20% for compaction.
  </p>
  <h3>Is a yard of dirt the same as a ton?</h3>
  <p>No. A cubic yard of dry fill weighs about 1.2 tons, so one ton is roughly 0.83 cubic yards.</p>
  <p>Mixing gravel, sand, or mulch on the same job? <a href="/cubic-yard-calculator/">Convert each area to cubic yards</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Compacted volume = area × depth. Loose volume to order = compacted volume × (1 + compaction %).</li>
    <li>Tons = loose cubic yards × weight per cubic yard, in US tons of 2,000 lb.</li>
    <li>Orders round up to the next half yard; truckloads are counted by volume and always round up.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
